          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-victoria-3fa9da6674c8bd2e\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.9,\"analysis\":\"Johannes privately avows that he still loves Victoria, strongly supporting T. The self-directed whisper, outstretched hands, and immediate repetition also give moderate independent support to E: the words function partly as an affective discharge when he is no longer speaking with her. Nothing indicates a commitment or relational undertaking, so P is unsupported.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The E evidence is meaningful but not decisive: the episode can also be read as a highly emotional private avowal whose force remains predominantly truth-conditional.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A directly narrated but privately spoken utterance: after Victoria walks away, Johannes whispers “I still love you, love you” to himself rather than addressing it audibly to her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration follows a guarded, wounded exchange in which Johannes and Victoria separate without openly acknowledging their attachment. Once she is walking away, Johannes contradicts his outward bitterness in a private avowal of continuing love. His reaching toward her and repeating the phrase suggest emotional overflow, but he does not use the declaration to undertake a commitment or alter their relationship; he calls only her name aloud afterward.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“jeg elsker Dem endnu, elsker Dem” (“I still love you, love you”).\",\"supports\":\"The present-tense declaration, especially “endnu” (‘still’), explicitly presents Johannes’s continuing love as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Han strakte hænderne ut og hvisket, sa ømme ord for sig selv” (“He stretched out his hands and whispered, speaking tender words to himself”).\",\"supports\":\"The noncommunicative self-address and embodied reaching provide moderate evidence that producing the words partly serves as expressive affective discharge, supporting E independently of mere passionate delivery.\",\"confidence\":0.86},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After the private declaration, he calls “Victoria!”; she turns but continues walking.\",\"supports\":\"Victoria does not receive the love declaration itself, and Johannes makes no promise or relational undertaking, weighing against P.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before parting, their dialogue is defensive and mutually wounded: “My impressions are as immaterial to you now as they were before.”\",\"supports\":\"The contrast between Johannes’s guarded public speech and subsequent private declaration frames the latter as a sincere-seeming avowal and emotional release.\",\"confidence\":0.91}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the explicit avowal of enduring love, while a moderate E score captures the self-directed expressive overflow. P is unnecessary, and no important aspect of the core force falls outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The analytical translation was used only to aid interpretation; quoted evidence is grounded in the supplied Norwegian source text.\"}"
        }
